1. Study group- Ishika, Simon, Morium

2. I filtered the dataset to adults between the ages 25–39, hoping there would be more variation in marriage rates. 
For demographics, I looked at income, children, and the relationship between income and gender.

For marriage rates by income:
I divided respondents into four income groups: lowest, lower-middle, upper-middle, and highest income using the quantiles functions in r.
Partnering rates increase with age for all four income groups.
At younger ages, the differences between income groups are relatively small.
By the late 30s, roughly 90% of the highest-income group is partnered, compared with only about 55–60% of the lowest-income group.
The relationship is especially noticeable after about age 30.

For marriage rates by children in household:
At age 25, about 45% of people with children are partnered, compared with about 20% of people without children.
The gap remains large across the entire age range.
By the late 30s, partnering reaches almost 90% among people with children, compared with approximately 50% among people without children.

For marriage rates by income and gender: 
For both males and females, partnering rates increase with age and are generally higher among higher-income respondents.
One interesting result is that the income differences appear larger than the male-female differences.
The trans and other groups show much more variation from age to age. Likely due to sample size.
